.TH "MAW-GETTING-STARTED" "7" "" "maw 0.2.4" "maw manual"
.SH NAME
maw-getting-started \- making a repo, building, activating
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A maw repo holds everything about your machine. This chapter makes one, renders it, and puts it in place: \fBinit\fR, \fBbuild\fR, and \fBactivate\fR, the three steps everything else builds on.
.SH "SETTING UP"
.PP
.EX
maw init ~/dotfiles
.EE
.PP
This creates the repo, or fills in whatever an existing one is missing, and records its path in \fB~/.config/maw/dotfiles\fR. Existing files are never overwritten.
.PP
.EX
~/dotfiles/
  default.nix    # entry point, leave as is
  config.nix     # your shared values
  maw.nix        # written by maw
  modules/       # one <name>.nix per program
  static/        # verbatim files
  out/           # rendered output, a dir per machine, committed with the repo
.EE
.PP
Every other command finds the repo through that recorded path, so it works from any directory.
.PP
Moving an existing setup in? Follow \fBmaw help migrating\fR instead.
.SS "On a new machine"
.PP
.EX
maw init https://github.com/you/dotfiles          # clones to ~/dotfiles
maw init git@github.com:you/dotfiles.git ~/dots   # or anywhere
.EE
.PP
Given a git url, \fBinit\fR clones the repo, prints the plan of what activating would do, and asks \fBactivate now? [Y/n]\fR. Saying yes installs every declared package, links and copies every file, and enables every service: the machine becomes the one the repo describes.
.PP
Nix isn't needed for that. If it isn't installed, maw activates from the committed \fBout/<machine>/\fR and its index, \fBout/<machine>/.maw/index.json\fR, which every activation keeps up to date; name the new machine after one that has been activated with nix (see \fBmaw help machines\fR). To do the same by hand: \fBmaw activate --no-build\fR. Install nix (\fBmaw install nix\fR) before editing modules; without it maw can't render them.
.SH "BUILDING"
.PP
.EX
maw build
.EE
.PP
Renders every module into \fBout/<machine>/<name>/\fR (each machine renders into its own dir, since machines can differ), named after the file's destination: \fBout/laptop/niri/config.kdl\fR, \fBout/laptop/waybar/style.css\fR. Output lists what happened:
.PP
.EX
eval niri
write out/laptop/niri/config.kdl
remove out/laptop/foot/foot.ini
.EE
.PP
\fBup to date\fR means nothing needed doing.
.PP
Builds are incremental:
.IP \(bu 2
A module is re-evaluated only when its file, \fBconfig.nix\fR, \fBmaw.nix\fR, or maw itself changed. Editing \fBconfig.nix\fR re-evaluates every module.
.IP \(bu 2
A file in \fBout/\fR is rewritten only when its content changed.
.IP \(bu 2
Files in \fBout/\fR that no module produces anymore are deleted.
.IP \(bu 2
A file in \fBout/\fR edited by hand (usually through its link, see drift) is never overwritten. \fBmaw build --force\fR backs up the edit and overwrites it.
.SH "ACTIVATING"
.PP
.EX
maw activate
.EE
.PP
Builds, then symlinks every file into place:
.IP \(bu 2
rendered files link to \fBout/\fR: \fB~/.config/niri/config.kdl -> ~/dotfiles/out/laptop/niri/config.kdl\fR
.IP \(bu 2
files in \fBstatic/<name>/\fR link as they are, each file on its own: \fBstatic/nvim/init.lua\fR goes to \fB~/.config/nvim/init.lua\fR
.PP
.EX
link ~/.config/niri/config.kdl
backup ~/.config/fuzzel/fuzzel.ini -> ~/.local/state/maw/backups/.config/fuzzel/fuzzel.ini
link ~/.config/fuzzel/fuzzel.ini
update ~/.config/waybar/style.css
unlink ~/.config/foot/foot.ini
set /org/gnome/desktop/interface/color-scheme 'prefer-dark'
reload waybar
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlink\fR: a new link. If a real file or someone else's link was already there, it's moved to the backups dir first (\fBbackup\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrelink\fR: the file now comes from somewhere else, e.g. it moved from a module to \fBstatic/\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\fR: the content behind an existing link changed. Nothing needs doing on disk.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBunlink\fR: a module or static file was removed, so its link is too. A backup made when it was first linked stays in the backups dir.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBset\fR / \fBreset\fR: a desktop setting declared with \fBmaw help modules\fR was written, or reset once nothing declares it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreload\fR: a program whose files changed was told to read them again, last, once everything is in place; see \fBmaw help modules\fR.
.PP
Since everything is a link, a change reaches the live file as soon as it's in \fBout/\fR or \fBstatic/\fR. Running \fBmaw activate\fR twice in a row does nothing the second time.
.SS "System files"
.PP
Files under \fB/\fR (a registry entry with \fBroot = true\fR, like greetd's \fB/etc/greetd/config.toml\fR) are copied with \fBsudo\fR instead of linked, since \fB/home\fR may not be mounted when the system reads them at boot:
.PP
.EX
backup /etc/greetd/config.toml -> ~/.local/state/maw/backups/system/etc/greetd/config.toml
copy /etc/greetd/config.toml
delete /etc/old/thing.conf
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcopy\fR: the file is new, or changed in the repo. The first time maw replaces a file it didn't write, the original is saved first (\fBbackup\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdelete\fR: nothing declares it anymore. A file someone edited since maw copied it is left alone.
.PP
A copy edited by hand is drift, like an edited link: reported as \fBedited\fR, left alone, and overwritten (after a backup) only with \fB--force\fR.
.PP
\fBmaw activate --dry-run\fR prints the plan and changes nothing, not even \fBout/\fR. \fBmaw activate --no-build\fR skips nix and activates what's committed in \fBout/\fR; see on a new machine.
.SS "Loose static files"
.PP
Files at the top of \fBstatic/\fR, outside any \fB<name>/\fR dir, are placed by type:
.PP
.EX
file                         goes to
executable                   ~/.local/bin/
image (png, jpg, webp, ...)  ~/.local/share/wallpapers/
font (ttf, otf, woff, ...)   ~/.local/share/fonts/
.EE
.PP
Anything else, or a file that fits more than one row, gets a question the first time you activate in a terminal:
.PP
.EX
static/notes.txt: [s]cript, [w]allpaper, [f]ont, or a path: ~/notes.txt
record notes.txt in maw.nix
.EE
.PP
A path works like a registry path: relative paths are under \fB~/.config\fR, and a path ending in \fB/\fR is a directory. The answer is recorded in \fBmaw.nix\fR \fBpaths\fR, so you're asked only once. An empty answer, or a run without a terminal, skips the file for now.
.SS "Drift"
.PP
maw never overwrites a change it didn't make. It reports two kinds:
.PP
.EX
drift ~/.config/niri/config.kdl: replaced by another file; --force to relink
drift ~/.config/waybar/style.css: edited in place; --force to overwrite
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreplaced\fR: the link is gone and something else sits there, often an app that saves by writing a new file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBedited\fR: the file was edited through its link, so \fBout/\fR holds your edit instead of what the module renders.
.PP
Desktop settings declared with \fBmaw help modules\fR drift the same way: \fBdrift /org/gnome/desktop/interface/color-scheme: changed since maw set it; --force to overwrite\fR.
.PP
Drifted files are left alone and reported on every run. To keep the change, move it into the module or \fBstatic/\fR. To discard it, run \fBmaw activate --force\fR, which backs up the drifted file and puts maw's version back.
.PP
Editing a \fBstatic/\fR file through its link is not drift: the link points straight at \fBstatic/\fR, so you're editing the repo.
.PP
